From d4ed6de6bbfe498dcf3fcf4d644500802f3f165f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Wed, 28 Oct 2026 19:01:16 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 1/3] camera: build maximum FPS from apertar-hardware.conf, 4K
 SDR up to the sensor probe

Why: the Mode Ladder (T34) runs 4K SDR 12-bit above 30 fps, up to what
the storage holds. Core capped 4K SDR at 30 although the IMX585 probe
reports 60, and had no build-wide limit. The WebUI, the touch UI and
the PCF list each kept their own FPS table, so none of them knew Core's
real ceiling.
What:
- apertar-hardware.conf gets a [camera] block with max_fps (empty in
  the template: no build limit). cameraMaxFpsFromHardwareConfig() reads
  the first existing candidate file like loadCfeHatConfig(); a number
  in 0 < fps <= 280 (decimals allowed, inline # comment stripped) is
  the build maximum, anything else means none. The App applies it
  before camera_.start() and after a successful Config save.
- CameraController::setBuildMaximumFps() stores it next to the probe
  globals. maximumSupportedFpsForResolution() returns at most that, so
  sanitizeSettingsForSensor(), set_fps, the ramp_fps stepper and H.264
  stop there; --simulate (no sensor table) applies it too. Outside a
  take a higher current FPS is lowered once, keeping the shutter angle
  as set_fps does; no reconfigure. A take keeps its rate.
- IMX585 4K SDR: 60 fps when the sensor probe ran, so the probe's own
  maximum wins through the existing min; 30 without a probe. Clear HDR
  is unchanged (16-bit 25; 12-bit 30 with the probe, 25 without).
- New derived CaptureSettings::fpsMax (not persisted): the highest FPS
  set_fps accepts outside a take, i.e. what the sanitiser leaves of 280
  in the current settings. Filled in settings() and
  emitSettingsEventLocked(), sent as "fps_max" by settingsEvent,
  settingsBody and buildWebStateJson. It does not change at record
  start.
- The PCF pot and fps_up/fps_down list ends at fps_max.
- WebUI: maximumSupportedFpsForSnapshot() returns fps_max when Core
  sends it (its table stays for an older Core); command replies merge
  fps_max and clear_hdr into the snapshot.
With max_fps=50 this also caps 1080p, the 3840 and 1920 crops (60
today) and H.264 at 50.

Tested: hostcheck-core.sh (g++ -fsyntax-only -Wall -Wextra with the
libcamera-apertar headers): CameraController.cpp, ApertarCoreApp.cpp
and WebControlPage.cpp errors=0 warnings=0; node --check on the page
script; cameraMaxFpsFromHardwareConfig() copied verbatim into a host
test without libcamera, 25 of 25 cases (no file, the template, decimals,
inline #, upper case, 0/negative/281/nan/inf/text give 0, other
sections, last key wins, CRLF). Not run on a Pi.

